('learn-aptitude-number-systems-core',1,'learn-aptitude-number-systems','Prime and composite numbers',0,'published','A prime is an integer greater than 1 with exactly two positive divisors. A composite has more than two.','Try dividing by primes no greater than the square root; any larger factor must have a smaller partner.','For 29, test 2, 3 and 5. None divides it, so 29 is prime.','1 is neither prime nor composite.','Explain prime and composite numbers in your own words, then give a concrete example and one limitation.','A prime is an integer greater than 1 with exactly two positive divisors. A composite has more than two. Try dividing by primes no greater than the square root; any larger factor must have a smaller partner.',null,'https://openstax.org/details/books/prealgebra-2e','PrepCraft original editorial','Technical reference check; no external peer review','2026-10-25',null),
('learn-aptitude-percentages-core',1,'learn-aptitude-percentages','Successive percentage change',0,'published','A percentage is a proportion of a stated base. Successive changes multiply their growth factors.','Track the base after each change rather than adding percentages.','100 × 1.2 × 0.8 = 96. A 20% rise followed by a 20% fall loses 4%.','Equal percentage increases and decreases do not cancel.','Explain successive percentage change in your own words, then give a concrete example and one limitation.','A percentage is a proportion of a stated base. Successive changes multiply their growth factors. Track the base after each change rather than adding percentages.',null,'https://openstax.org/details/books/prealgebra-2e','PrepCraft original editorial','Technical reference check; no external peer review','2026-10-25',null),
('learn-aptitude-ratio-proportion-core',1,'learn-aptitude-ratio-proportion','Dividing in a ratio',0,'published','A ratio describes relative amounts; it does not specify the total.','Add the parts, find one part, then scale.','Split 600 in the ratio 2:3: five parts mean 120 per part; the shares are 240 and 360.','The first share of a 2:3 split is 2/5, not 2/3, of the total.','Explain dividing in a ratio in your own words, then give a concrete example and one limitation.','A ratio describes relative amounts; it does not specify the total. Add the parts, find one part, then scale.',null,'https://openstax.org/details/books/prealgebra-2e','PrepCraft original editorial','Technical reference check; no external peer review','2026-10-25',null),
('learn-aptitude-time-work-core',1,'learn-aptitude-time-work','Combined work rates',0,'published','Work rates add when workers independently contribute to the same task.','Use a common job unit per day. Add fractions of a job, not completion times.','A needs 6 days and B needs 3. Their daily rates are 1/6 and 1/3; together 1/2, so they need 2 days.','Adding completion times gives the wrong unit and result.','Explain combined work rates in your own words, then give a concrete example and one limitation.','Work rates add when workers independently contribute to the same task. Use a common job unit per day. Add fractions of a job, not completion times.',null,'https://openstax.org/details/books/prealgebra-2e','PrepCraft original editorial','Technical reference check; no external peer review','2026-10-25',null),
('learn-aptitude-speed-time-distance-core',1,'learn-aptitude-speed-time-distance','Average speed',0,'published','Average speed is total distance divided by total elapsed time.','First add distances and times, then divide. Equal distances and equal times behave differently.','Travel 60 km at 30 km/h and 60 km at 60 km/h: 120 km in 3 hours gives 40 km/h.','The arithmetic mean of speeds works for equal durations, not equal distances.','Explain average speed in your own words, then give a concrete example and one limitation.','Average speed is total distance divided by total elapsed time. First add distances and times, then divide. Equal distances and equal times behave differently.',null,'https://openstax.org/details/books/prealgebra-2e','PrepCraft original editorial','Technical reference check; no external peer review','2026-10-25',null),
('learn-java-fundamentals-core',1,'learn-java-fundamentals','Java values and references',0,'published','Java passes argument values. For an object variable, that value is a reference to an object.','Copying a reference lets two variables reach one object. Reassigning one local variable does not reassign the caller’s variable.','A method can mutate a supplied list, but assigning its parameter to a new list does not replace the caller’s list.','Java does not pass caller variables by reference.','Explain java values and references in your own words, then give a concrete example and one limitation.','Java passes argument values. For an object variable, that value is a reference to an object. Copying a reference lets two variables reach one object. Reassigning one local variable does not reassign the caller’s variable.','runtime','https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/','PrepCraft original editorial','Technical reference check; no external peer review','2026-10-25','Java SE 25'),
('learn-java-oop-core',1,'learn-java-oop','Overriding and dynamic dispatch',0,'published','Overriding supplies an instance method implementation in a subtype. Runtime dispatch selects the implementation for the actual object.','Separate the declared reference type from the runtime object type.','Animal a = new Dog(); a.speak() calls Dog.speak() when it correctly overrides the accessible instance method.','Static method hiding is not instance method overriding.','Explain overriding and dynamic dispatch in your own words, then give a concrete example and one limitation.','Overriding supplies an instance method implementation in a subtype. Runtime dispatch selects the implementation for the actual object. Separate the declared reference type from the runtime object type.',null,'https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/','PrepCraft original editorial','Technical reference check; no external peer review','2026-10-25','Java SE 25'),
('learn-java-collections-framework-core',1,'learn-java-collections-framework','Choosing a collection',0,'published','Collections express ordering, duplication and lookup requirements. A Map associates keys with values; it is not a subtype of Collection.','Choose by the operations and invariants your program needs.','Use a HashMap to count words by key; use an ArrayList when ordered indexed traversal matters.','A HashMap has no promised encounter order.','Explain choosing a collection in your own words, then give a concrete example and one limitation.','Collections express ordering, duplication and lookup requirements. A Map associates keys with values; it is not a subtype of Collection. Choose by the operations and invariants your program needs.',null,'https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/','PrepCraft original editorial','Technical reference check; no external peer review','2026-10-25','Java SE 25'),
('learn-java-exceptions-core',1,'learn-java-exceptions','Checked and unchecked exceptions',0,'published','Checked exceptions are subject to compile-time catch-or-declare rules. RuntimeException subclasses and Error subclasses are unchecked.','Exception handling is a control-flow contract; catch the exceptions you can meaningfully handle.','Reading a file may throw IOException. Use try-with-resources for an AutoCloseable resource.','A finally block should not return a value: it can hide an earlier exception or result.','Explain checked and unchecked exceptions in your own words, then give a concrete example and one limitation.','Checked exceptions are subject to compile-time catch-or-declare rules. RuntimeException subclasses and Error subclasses are unchecked. Exception handling is a control-flow contract; catch the exceptions you can meaningfully handle.',null,'https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/','PrepCraft original editorial','Technical reference check; no external peer review','2026-10-25','Java SE 25'),
('learn-java-generics-core',1,'learn-java-generics','PECS and wildcard variance',0,'published','A producer extends a type; a consumer uses a super bound. Java generic types are invariant.','A List<? extends Number> produces Number values but its unknown element subtype prevents safely adding an arbitrary Number.','Copy from List<? extends Number> into List<? super Number> when those bounds match the operation.','List<Integer> is not a subtype of List<Number>.','Explain pecs and wildcard variance in your own words, then give a concrete example and one limitation.','A producer extends a type; a consumer uses a super bound. Java generic types are invariant. A List<? extends Number> produces Number values but its unknown element subtype prevents safely adding an arbitrary Number.',null,'https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/','PrepCraft original editorial','Technical reference check; no external peer review','2026-10-25','Java SE 25'),
('learn-sql-dbms-fundamentals-core',1,'learn-sql-dbms-fundamentals','SELECT and NULL',0,'published','SELECT constructs a result from relations. NULL represents an unknown or absent value; comparisons with it are not ordinary Boolean equality.','Think in rows and predicates. WHERE keeps rows whose predicate is true.','SELECT name FROM people WHERE manager_id IS NULL returns people with no known manager.','Use IS NULL, not = NULL.','Explain select and null in your own words, then give a concrete example and one limitation.','SELECT constructs a result from relations. NULL represents an unknown or absent value; comparisons with it are not ordinary Boolean equality. Think in rows and predicates. WHERE keeps rows whose predicate is true.',null,'https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/tutorial.html','PrepCraft original editorial','Technical reference check; no external peer review','2026-10-25',null),
('learn-sql-dbms-joins-core',1,'learn-sql-dbms-joins','LEFT JOIN and missing matches',0,'published','A LEFT JOIN retains every left row, adding matching right values or NULLs when no match exists.','Separate the matching condition in ON from filtering the joined result in WHERE.','Customers LEFT JOIN orders ON orders.customer_id = customers.id retains customers with no orders.','A right-side WHERE predicate can discard unmatched rows and change the intended outer-join behavior.','Explain left join and missing matches in your own words, then give a concrete example and one limitation.','A LEFT JOIN retains every left row, adding matching right values or NULLs when no match exists. Separate the matching condition in ON from filtering the joined result in WHERE.',null,'https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/tutorial.html','PrepCraft original editorial','Technical reference check; no external peer review','2026-10-25',null),
('learn-sql-dbms-aggregation-core',1,'learn-sql-dbms-aggregation','GROUP BY and HAVING',0,'published','Grouping collects rows sharing a key; aggregate functions summarize each group. HAVING filters groups.','WHERE applies before grouping, HAVING after aggregation.','SELECT team, COUNT(*) FROM members GROUP BY team HAVING COUNT(*) >= 3 finds teams with at least three members.','COUNT(column) skips NULL values; COUNT(*) counts rows.','Explain group by and having in your own words, then give a concrete example and one limitation.','Grouping collects rows sharing a key; aggregate functions summarize each group. HAVING filters groups. WHERE applies before grouping, HAVING after aggregation.',null,'https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/tutorial.html','PrepCraft original editorial','Technical reference check; no external peer review','2026-10-25',null),
('learn-sql-dbms-normalization-core',1,'learn-sql-dbms-normalization','Functional dependencies and 3NF',0,'published','A functional dependency X → Y means equal X values determine equal Y values. Normalization reduces dependency-driven redundancy.','Put facts with the key that determines them, while checking lossless decomposition and dependency preservation.','If employee_id determines department_id and department_id determines department_name, storing department_name per employee repeats a department fact.','More tables do not automatically imply better normalization or faster queries.','Explain functional dependencies and 3nf in your own words, then give a concrete example and one limitation.','A functional dependency X → Y means equal X values determine equal Y values. Normalization reduces dependency-driven redundancy. Put facts with the key that determines them, while checking lossless decomposition and dependency preservation.',null,'https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/tutorial.html','PrepCraft original editorial','Technical reference check; no external peer review','2026-10-25',null),
('learn-sql-dbms-transactions-core',1,'learn-sql-dbms-transactions','Lost updates and isolation',0,'published','Concurrent transactions can overwrite each other’s read-modify-write work. Isolation controls permitted observations and interleavings.','Write down both transactions in time order. Ask what each read and which changes survived.','Both read 100. A writes 80, then B writes 70 based on its stale 100. A’s debit can be lost.','The exact anomalies prevented at each isolation level depend on the database engine.','Explain lost updates and isolation in your own words, then give a concrete example and one limitation.','Concurrent transactions can overwrite each other’s read-modify-write work. Isolation controls permitted observations and interleavings. Write down both transactions in time order. Ask what each read and which changes survived.','transaction','https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/tutorial.html','PrepCraft original editorial','Technical reference check; no external peer review','2026-10-25',null),
('learn-operating-systems-processes-core',1,'learn-operating-systems-processes','Process address spaces',0,'published','A process is an executing program with resources and an address-space abstraction. Threads within it share many resources.','Treat a process as a resource boundary and a thread as an execution stream.','Two processes normally have separate virtual address spaces; communicating needs an explicit mechanism.','A program file is not itself a running process.','Explain process address spaces in your own words, then give a concrete example and one limitation.','A process is an executing program with resources and an address-space abstraction. Threads within it share many resources. Treat a process as a resource boundary and a thread as an execution stream.',null,'https://pages.cs.wisc.edu/~remzi/OSTEP/','PrepCraft original editorial','Technical reference check; no external peer review','2026-10-25',null),
('learn-operating-systems-threads-core',1,'learn-operating-systems-threads','Shared state and thread safety',0,'published','Threads in a process share its address space while keeping their own execution context, including stacks.','Concurrent access to shared mutable state needs a correctness argument.','Two threads incrementing a non-atomic counter can lose updates despite sharing the same variable.','Sharing an address space does not make compound operations atomic.','Explain shared state and thread safety in your own words, then give a concrete example and one limitation.','Threads in a process share its address space while keeping their own execution context, including stacks. Concurrent access to shared mutable state needs a correctness argument.',null,'https://pages.cs.wisc.edu/~remzi/OSTEP/','PrepCraft original editorial','Technical reference check; no external peer review','2026-10-25',null),
('learn-operating-systems-cpu-scheduling-core',1,'learn-operating-systems-cpu-scheduling','Scheduling and waiting time',0,'published','A scheduler chooses which ready work runs next. Waiting time measures time spent ready but not executing.','Draw a Gantt timeline before computing completion, turnaround and waiting times.','For FCFS with P1 arrival 0 burst 5, P2 arrival 1 burst 3, P3 arrival 2 burst 1: intervals are P1 0–5, P2 5–8, P3 8–9.','Non-preemptive SJF chooses among arrived ready jobs, not future jobs.','Explain scheduling and waiting time in your own words, then give a concrete example and one limitation.','A scheduler chooses which ready work runs next. Waiting time measures time spent ready but not executing. Draw a Gantt timeline before computing completion, turnaround and waiting times.','scheduler','https://pages.cs.wisc.edu/~remzi/OSTEP/','PrepCraft original editorial','Technical reference check; no external peer review','2026-10-25',null),
('learn-operating-systems-synchronization-core',1,'learn-operating-systems-synchronization','Mutex and critical sections',0,'published','A critical section accesses shared state that must satisfy an invariant. A mutex can serialize that section.','Protect the whole invariant, including every related read and write.','Checking a stock count and decrementing it must be one coordinated operation when two threads buy concurrently.','A lock only helps if every conflicting access follows the same discipline.','Explain mutex and critical sections in your own words, then give a concrete example and one limitation.','A critical section accesses shared state that must satisfy an invariant. A mutex can serialize that section. Protect the whole invariant, including every related read and write.',null,'https://pages.cs.wisc.edu/~remzi/OSTEP/','PrepCraft original editorial','Technical reference check; no external peer review','2026-10-25',null),
('learn-operating-systems-deadlocks-core',1,'learn-operating-systems-deadlocks','Deadlock conditions',0,'published','Deadlock can occur when mutual exclusion, hold-and-wait, no preemption and circular wait coexist.','Look for a cycle of resources held while waiting for another.','Thread A holds L1 and waits for L2; B holds L2 and waits for L1. A consistent lock order removes this cycle.','Being slow or temporarily blocked is not sufficient evidence of deadlock.','Explain deadlock conditions in your own words, then give a concrete example and one limitation.','Deadlock can occur when mutual exclusion, hold-and-wait, no preemption and circular wait coexist. Look for a cycle of resources held while waiting for another.',null,'https://pages.cs.wisc.edu/~remzi/OSTEP/','PrepCraft original editorial','Technical reference check; no external peer review','2026-10-25',null),
('learn-networks-tcp-core',1,'learn-networks-tcp','TCP three-way handshake',0,'published','TCP establishes a connection by exchanging SYN, SYN-ACK and ACK while synchronizing sequence numbers.','Each side must learn the other’s initial sequence number and acknowledge it.','Client sends SYN(seq=x), server SYN-ACK(seq=y, ack=x+1), client ACK(ack=y+1).','A TCP connection does not itself provide encryption; TLS is a separate protocol.','Explain tcp three-way handshake in your own words, then give a concrete example and one limitation.','TCP establishes a connection by exchanging SYN, SYN-ACK and ACK while synchronizing sequence numbers. Each side must learn the other’s initial sequence number and acknowledge it.','request','https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP','PrepCraft original editorial','Technical reference check; no external peer review','2026-10-25',null),
('learn-networks-dns-core',1,'learn-networks-dns','DNS resolution and caching',0,'published','DNS maps names to records through a distributed hierarchy. Resolvers can cache answers according to TTLs.','Separate the browser’s lookup, the recursive resolver and authoritative information.','A resolver may answer from cache. Otherwise it can follow referrals toward an authoritative server.','DNS resolution does not require contacting every hierarchy level for every request.','Explain dns resolution and caching in your own words, then give a concrete example and one limitation.','DNS maps names to records through a distributed hierarchy. Resolvers can cache answers according to TTLs. Separate the browser’s lookup, the recursive resolver and authoritative information.',null,'https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP','PrepCraft original editorial','Technical reference check; no external peer review','2026-10-25',null),
('learn-networks-http-core',1,'learn-networks-http','HTTP method semantics',0,'published','Safe methods are intended to read without requesting state changes. Idempotent methods have the same intended effect when repeated.','Distinguish intended server effect from whether responses or logs are identical.','Repeated DELETE for one resource can return different statuses while remaining idempotent in its intended effect.','Idempotent does not mean every response is identical.','Explain http method semantics in your own words, then give a concrete example and one limitation.','Safe methods are intended to read without requesting state changes. Idempotent methods have the same intended effect when repeated. Distinguish intended server effect from whether responses or logs are identical.',null,'https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP','PrepCraft original editorial','Technical reference check; no external peer review','2026-10-25',null),
('learn-networks-https-tls-core',1,'learn-networks-https-tls','TLS and HTTPS',0,'published','HTTPS carries HTTP through TLS to provide authenticated encryption under the negotiated protocol and trust configuration.','Encryption and identity verification solve different parts of connection security.','The client checks the certificate’s trust chain and hostname; negotiated traffic keys protect subsequent data.','A certificate is not a guarantee that the application is honest or bug-free.','Explain tls and https in your own words, then give a concrete example and one limitation.','HTTPS carries HTTP through TLS to provide authenticated encryption under the negotiated protocol and trust configuration. Encryption and identity verification solve different parts of connection security.',null,'https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP','PrepCraft original editorial','Technical reference check; no external peer review','2026-10-25',null),
('learn-networks-cookies-sessions-core',1,'learn-networks-cookies-sessions','Cookies and server sessions',0,'published','A cookie is data managed by the browser under cookie rules. A server session often uses an opaque cookie identifier to find server-side state.','Separate the browser-held identifier from the server-held session information.','HttpOnly limits script access to a cookie; Secure restricts its transmission to secure connections in normal operation.','HttpOnly does not make an application immune to CSRF or all consequences of XSS.','Explain cookies and server sessions in your own words, then give a concrete example and one limitation.','A cookie is data managed by the browser under cookie rules. A server session often uses an opaque cookie identifier to find server-side state. Separate the browser-held identifier from the server-held session information.',null,'https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP','PrepCraft original editorial','Technical reference check; no external peer review','2026-10-25',null);
